From: seilent <github@seilent.net>
Date: Sat, 30 May 2026 03:05:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: qcom: tsens: do not arm TSENS IRQs as wakeup sources

The TSENS sensor interrupts are level-triggered and registered as system
wakeup sources via enable_irq_wake(). On SM8550 (AYANEO Pocket ACE) this
makes the device wake itself from s2idle almost immediately whenever the
SoC is warm: as it sleeps and cools, the temperature crosses a trip and
the level IRQ asserts continuously, producing a wake storm (observed
~1900 IRQ 23 events in ~15 s, waking the device in well under the test's
RTC backstop). It manifests as "the device turns on by itself" after
gaming, and was previously misattributed to the power button / Steam.

A handheld does not need a thermal interrupt to wake the AP from sleep:
the PMIC performs emergency thermal shutdown autonomously in hardware,
and the power key remains a wakeup source for the user. Drop the
enable_irq_wake() call so TSENS interrupts still drive thermal
throttling while running, but no longer wake the system from suspend.

Signed-off-by: seilent <github@seilent.net>
---
 drivers/thermal/qcom/tsens.c | 2 --
 1 file changed, 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thermal/qcom/tsens.c b/drivers/thermal/qcom/tsens.c
--- a/drivers/thermal/qcom/tsens.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/qcom/tsens.c
@@ -1204,8 +1204,6 @@ static int tsens_register_irq(struct tsens_priv *priv, char *irqname,
 		if (ret)
 			dev_err(&pdev->dev, "%s: failed to get irq\n",
 				__func__);
-		else
-			enable_irq_wake(irq);
 	}
 
 	put_device(&pdev->dev);
